  to do this we have special kinematic  variables which  turn out to be very useful the most  important one is probably this x here  which is called the bjergen  scaling x of broken x which  you can think about so this is q square  as a momentum transfer of the photon  p is the momentum of the proton q is the  momentum transfer is q here  factor of two and what this is is  basically the fraction of the momentum  carried by the part on  here in the scattering process there's a  few other useful variables but i don't  want to go into any of the details here  so there's a number of very important  scattering experiments  um the first one i mentioned before is a  slag mit experiment  which led to the discovery that the  proton is made out of quarks  and the nobel prize in physics 1990 to  jerry friedman henry kendall  and taylor and what they did is they  basically had a beam  um at slack of electrons of 5 to 20 gev  and they scattered this beam of a  hydrogen target protons  and they use this spectrometer here in  order to then make a differential  measurement of the scattered electrons  so that's very cool um even higher  energies were available at hera  um the electron positron collider where  the energies of the electrons were in  the order of 30 gb  of the protons up to 3 830 gb  um and so what we find then in those  collisions of differential cross-section  measurement is shown here and it's a  very it's not an easy plot to read  so you see our structure function here  in the logarithmic plot  remember this is a log template here  and you see here q square the momentum  transfer of the photon so the energy  used in the scattering process  when we try to read this here we can  look at a fixed q square for example  and at a fixed q square you see that  um if you if you  probe if you're testing for a fixed  fraction of the patterns taking away  patons fraction of the partner's  momentum  of the proton you see that the lower the  fractions the more particles you see  