entum  distributions of the particles in the  protons  and so we come back to how we use this  later but i want to introduce part-time  distribution functions which do exactly  that  they're defined as a probability to find  a part on in the proton that carries  energy between  x and x plus dx you can write them  [Music]  using the structure functions before but  they literally describe  this probability and so what you find  inside the protons are now the valence  clock  the down clock and the up quarks c  quarks and antiquarks  influence so you want to describe those  momentum  distributions or energy distributions of  those particles  there's a number of sum rules uh if you  integrate momentum fractions from zero  to one  one being the momentum of the proton if  you integrate them all together you have  to find one  because that is the momentum of the  proton to start with  if you integrate the down quarks in the  up quarks you find  one or two meaning that those are the  number of valence corks we have  available  if you integrate the distributions of  strange and anti-strange and charm and  anti-charm  you get zero well because there needs to  be the same number of  uh strange and anti-strange and charming  anti-strange  um and because of energy conservation  the sum needs to this sum so sums need  to be zero  and then we can look at those so what's  shown here is x  times the pattern distribution functions  the pdf  as a function of f and what you see here  for our valence quarks you see a  distribution which is kind of what you  expect  it almost peaks at 0.3 a third um and  has a distributions because there's  kinematics involved  and also um interactions because of the  interactions with  the clones and then you see  the the c quarks and antiquarks and you  see them  increasing in numbers quite  significantly as you go to small  fraction of the momentum carriage  it's exactly what we just discussed in  the previous plot already  an interesting way to look a